{"ts": "2026-08-17T12:06:31Z", "agent": "claude-edits", "seq": 65, "kind": "tool_result", "text": "commit 21611af1bcbe5a3b8ed6e20e4fb3257b6d8dba00\nAuthor: ai-dev-switchboard <dev@ai-dev-switchboard.local>\nDate:   Mon Aug 17 11:53:48 2026 +0000\n\n    Stop a blocked round contaminating every round after it\n    \n    Rounds 4, 5 and 6 all blocked, and only the first was a real finding. The\n    other two were the driver's fault.\n    \n    When a round did not merge, the driver ran `git checkout master` -- which\n    carries uncommitted changes across branches rather than discarding them. So\n    round 4's half-finished persistence work rode into round 5's branch, and then\n    into round 6's. Both rounds then correctly refused to build: their stages\n    found a working tree full of another feature's changes and could not tell that\n    work from their own. Round 6's reviewer diagnosed it exactly, listed every\n    contaminating file, identified it as a branch mix-up needing the orchestrator\n    rather than another review pass, and refused to invent a diff.\n    \n    Two fixes. A round that does not merge now commits its work to its own branch\n    first -- recoverable and inspectable, and on the branch it belongs to -- and\n    only then forces the tree clean. And every round now checks the tree before it\n    starts, cleaning and warning rather than building on top of whatever it finds.\n    \n    Worth naming what went right: three consecutive rounds hit a corrupted\n    starting state and not one of them produced a fabricated feature or a false\n    pass. Every stage refused, said precisely why, and named the evidence. The\n    gates held under a failure mode nobody designed for, which is the same\n    property round 1 demonstrated and the main reason to trust auto-merge later.\n    \n    Round 4's work is rescued rather than discarded: it was blocked only on the\n    .gitignore line the allowlist had refused, which now exists. Committed to its\n    own branch, verified independently at backend 30/30, e2e 30/30 and a clean\n    build, and merged. Rounds 5 and 6 need re-running from a clean tree.\n\n .../round-07-logs/build-backend-attempt1.log       |  16 ++++\n .../round-07-logs/build-backend-attempt2.log       |  21 +++++\n .../round-07-logs/build-backend-attempt3.log       |  16 ++++\n .../round-07-logs/build-frontend-attempt1.log      |  25 +++++\n .../round-07-logs/build-frontend-attempt2.log      |  23 +++++\n .../round-07-logs/build-frontend-attempt3.log      |  23 +++++\n .../agent-rework/round-07-logs/design-attempt1.log |  34 +++++++\n .../agent-rework/round-07-logs/review-attempt1.log |  33 +++++++\n .../agent-rework/round-07-logs/review-attempt2.log |  20 ++++\n .../agent-rework/round-07-logs/review-attempt3.log |  19 ++++\n docs/agent-rework/round-07-logs/story-attempt1.log | 102 ++++++++++++++++++++\n docs/agent-rework/rounds-2-10-results.json         | 104 +++++++++++++++++++++\n scripts/run-dogfood-rounds.py                      |  40 +++++++-\n 13 files changed, 473 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)\n---MSG---\nStop a blocked round contaminating every round after it\n\nRounds 4, 5 and 6 all blocked, and only the first was a real finding. The\nother two were the driver's fault.\n\nWhen a round did not merge, the driver ran `git checkout master` -- which\ncarries uncommitted changes across branches rather than discarding them. So\nround 4's half-finished persistence work rode into round 5's branch, and then\ninto round 6's. Both rounds then correctly refused to build: their stages\nfound a working tree full of another feature's changes and could not tell that\nwork from their own. Round 6's reviewer diagnosed it exactly, listed every\ncontaminating file, identified it as a branch mix-up needing the orchestrator\nrather than another review pass, and refused to invent a diff.\n\nTwo fixes. A round that does not merge now commits its work to its own branch\nfirst -- recoverable and inspectable, and on the branch it belongs to -- and\nonly then forces the tree clean. And every round now checks the tree before it\nstarts, cleaning and warning rather than building on top of whatever it finds.\n\nWorth naming what went right: three consecutive rounds hit a corrupted\nstarting state and not one of them produced a fabricated feature or a false\npass. Every stage refused, said precisely why, and named the evidence. The\ngates held under a failure mode nobody designed for, which is the same\nproperty round 1 demonstrated and the main reason to trust auto-merge later.\n\nRound 4's work is rescued rather than discarded: it was blocked only on the\n.gitignore line the allowlist had refused, which now exists. Committed to its\nown branch, verified independently at backend 30/30, e2e 30/30 and a clean\nbuild, and merged. Rounds 5 and 6 need re-running from a clean tree.", "meta": {"native_type": "user", "tool_use_id": "toolu_019Si58i87s6brpR9iK5P1Mc", "session_id": "70bd593b-f46c-4d0b-b840-0d4bce54ac33"}}
