{"ts": "2026-08-17T12:08:05Z", "agent": "claude-edits", "seq": 149, "kind": "tool_result", "text": "1\tAll four files match the design exactly. Handoff summary:\n2\t\n3\t## Frontend implementation \u2014 Taiga #9 done\n4\t\n5\t**Files changed:**\n6\t- `frontend/src/api.js:26` \u2014 added `clearCompleted = () => request('/api/tasks/completed', { method: 'DELETE' })`, same shape as the sibling exports.\n7\t- `frontend/src/components/ClearCompleted.jsx` \u2014 new file, exactly per `docs/design.md`: `<button disabled={count === 0}>Clear completed ({count})</button>`, no new state, text is the accessible name.\n8\t- `frontend/src/App.jsx` \u2014 added `completedCount = tasks.filter(t => t.done).length` (from the full `tasks` array, not `visibleTasks`, so it stays store-wide per AC4); wrapped `TaskFilter` + new `ClearCompleted` in a `.list-toolbar` div; `onClear` wired through the existing `run()` helper so failures surface via the existing `error` banner, same path as every other mutation.\n9\t- `frontend/src/App.css` \u2014 added `.list-toolbar` (flex, space-between, wraps at the narrow container width) and `.list-toolbar .task-filter { margin-bottom: 0 }` to cancel the doubled margin now that the toolbar owns it. No new button rule \u2014 `ClearCompleted` inherits the global `button`/`button:disabled` styling, consistent with \"no destructive-red convention\" noted in the design.\n10\t\n11\t**Scope note \u2014 depends on the backend half (out of my scope):** `api.clearCompleted()` calls `DELETE /api/tasks/completed`. That route, `TaskStore.clear_completed()`, and the `ClearResult` model are the backend developer's slice per `docs/spec.md`'s Affected areas \u2014 not touched here.\n12\t\n13\t**Verification gap \u2014 please read before trusting \"it builds\":** I could not run `npm run lint`, `npm run build`, or any `node`/`npm` invocation in this session \u2014 the Bash tool blocked every one of them with \"This command requires approval,\" and no user is available to approve in this non-interactive dispatch (only trivial builtins like `ls`/`echo` went through). I have **not** run the project's own type/lint/build check, which the developer role's discipline requires before claiming something works. Instead I did a manual line-by-line read of every changed file (`App.jsx`, `api.js`, `App.css`, `ClearCompleted.jsx`) against the design doc's exact snippets \u2014 imports resolve, JSX is balanced, prop names match (`count`/`onClear`), CSS selectors match existing class names (`.task-filter`) \u2014 but this is not a substitute for actually running `npm run build`. Please run `cd frontend && npm run lint && npm run build` before treating this as verified; I'd flag this as a hard blocker for a \"done\" claim if I had a way to unblock it myself, but the tool permission is outside developer-role authority to change.\n14\t\n15\tNo frontend test framework exists in this repo (`package.json` has no `test` script, `frontend/` has zero test files anywhere) \u2014 CLAUDE.md's documented `npm test` command doesn't correspond to any actual script, and neither `docs/spec.md` nor `docs/design.md` asked for new frontend tests for this ticket (all 6 ACs are either backend-testable or purely the `disabled={count === 0}` structural guarantee, which the design doc already reasons is a DOM-level guarantee, not something a rendered-output test adds confidence to here). I did not add a test harness \u2014 that would be scope creep beyond this ticket.", "meta": {"native_type": "user", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01HyrfeZHo9PYd5pCX774wUr", "session_id": "70bd593b-f46c-4d0b-b840-0d4bce54ac33"}}
