{"ts": "2026-08-17T12:09:05Z", "agent": "claude-edits", "seq": 186, "kind": "message", "text": "That's fine \u2014 the edit already applied cleanly per the Edit tool's confirmation. Here's the retrospective.\n\n## What happened\n\nRound 5 (Taiga #9, \"clear all completed tasks\") ran story \u2192 design \u2192 build/frontend + build/backend \u2192 review \u2192 build/frontend + build/backend \u2192 review \u2192 test, 9 dispatches, finished with an approved, tested feature. The first review pass correctly caught that the backend half was entirely missing; the second build pass added it and everything passed cleanly from there.\n\n## What cost time or quality\n\n`build-backend-attempt1` did the real work of establishing the plan \u2014 it read `store.py`, `main.py`, the existing tests, and the spec, and settled on an exact plan (\"implement `clear_completed()` in store.py, `ClearResult` model, and the route in main.py above line 56\" \u2014 `build-backend-attempt1.log`). It then hit two denied `Edit` calls to `backend/app/models.py` (\"Claude requested permissions to write to ... but you haven't granted it yet\"), and gave up with a single line: *\"I need the user to grant permission to edit files in this project before I can proceed.\"* (`build-backend-attempt1.output`).\n\nThat discarded everything it had just worked out. `build-backend-attempt2` had to reopen `store.py`, `main.py`, and the spec and re-derive the same plan from scratch before it could implement it (`build-backend-attempt2.output`: \"an earlier partial attempt had left some inconsistent/duplicated state... which I found and cleaned up\"). It also forced a full, otherwise-avoidable `changes-requested` review round-trip (`review-attempt1.output`), since the reviewer had nothing to look at but a one-line refusal.\n\nNotably, `agents/tester.md` already has a \"When a tool call is denied\" section instructing exactly this: don't retry identically, fall back and report the gap plainly \u2014 added after \"three rounds of retrospectives stalled exactly this way\" per its own text. `agents/developer.md` had no equivalent section, and this round shows the gap isn't just about retrying blindly \u2014 the deeper problem is discarding a completed analysis instead of handing it off.\n\n## What I changed\n\n`agents/developer.md` \u2014 added a \"When a tool call is denied\" section (mirroring `tester.md`'s pattern, adapted for Edit/Write denials specific to this role): when blocked, finish the plan and hand off the exact file/content/location instead of a bare refusal, so a retry can apply it directly rather than re-deriving it. Evidence: `build-backend-attempt1.log` + `.output` vs. the rediscovery visible in `build-backend-attempt2.output`.\n\n## What I deliberately did not change\n\n- The permission denial itself (why `Edit(./backend/**)` in `test-project/.claude/settings.json` was refused in attempt 1 but succeeded on the identical file in attempt 2) is a harness/permission-hook question, not a role-instruction question \u2014 outside my remit.\n- `build-frontend-attempt1`'s inability to run `npm run lint`/`npm run build` (not in the project's command allowlist): it already followed convention correctly \u2014 fell back to a manual line-by-line read and flagged the verification gap explicitly in its handoff (\"please run `cd frontend && npm run lint && npm run build` before treating this as verified\"). No instruction gap there.\n- `test-attempt1`'s comment that review-attempt1's \"changes-requested\" verdict looked stale: cosmetic misreading of the round's chronology, didn't affect its `pass` verdict. Not worth a second edit against this round's one-or-two-change budget.\n- The reviewer/tester loop-back mechanics worked exactly as designed: build failed, review caught it and refused to invent a diff, build fixed it, review and test both passed clean. No change warranted.\n\n## Needs a human\n\nThe Edit-permission denial pattern itself (`test-project/.claude/settings.json` allowlist vs. the actual PreToolUse hook behavior in headless dispatches) is worth a look if it recurs \u2014 it's machinery/environment, not something `agents/*.md` or `config/roles.d/*.role` can reach.\n\n```\nVERDICT: done\n```", "meta": {"native_type": "assistant", "block_type": "text", "session_id": "70bd593b-f46c-4d0b-b840-0d4bce54ac33"}}
